WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-nominated actor

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.170
you know from films
like "Nocturnal Animals"

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.439
and "Revolutionary Road,"

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.975
as well as the HBO series
"Boardwalk Empire."

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.044
He stars in the Showtime Limited
Series "George &amp; Tammy."

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:14.880
The finale airs January 8th.
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.218
-♪ He said, "I'll love you
till I die" ♪

00:00:19.218 --> 00:00:22.122
♪♪

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:26.092
♪ She told him,
"You'll forget in time ♪

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.894
♪♪

00:00:28.894 --> 00:00:33.900
♪ As the years went slowly by ♪

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.768
♪♪

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:40.139
♪ She still preyed
upon his mind ♪

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:43.175
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Michael Shannon,

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:45.211
everyone!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:48.214
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:54.287
♪♪

00:00:54.287 --> 00:01:00.326
♪♪

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.763
Welcome back!
-Thanks. Thanks, Seth.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.898
-First guest of 2023.
-Oh, wow.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.334
2024
-Yeah.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:11.170
[ Laughter ]
I'd get in that DeLorean.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.404
-You would?
-Yeah.

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:13.740
-You'd go
a year into the future?

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.175
-It was nice of you
to go to the party with him.

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:18.177
-Yeah.
I mean I worry about him.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:20.246
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-He used to be America's mayor,

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:21.981
and now I feel like he's
a little down on his luck.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.849
-And senile.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:25.818
Where would you go if
you had a time machine?

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:27.653
-Oh, dear Lord.

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:31.691
I would have liked to have been
here in New York in the '70s.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.659
-Yeah.
I think that people

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.394
who were actually here
in New York in the '70s

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:37.030
think we're crazy
for wanting that.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.465
-They do.
-But I kind of agree with you.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:42.168
-I would have gone to CBGB's
every night, you know?

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.435
-Yeah.
-That's the kind of guy I am.

00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:46.539
-It's interesting 'cause
that's what draws you --

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.141
that's the kind of music
you'd be drawn to, right?

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.142
New York in the '70s.
George Jones --

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:52.345
you seem like a natural,
but country music

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:53.947
was not part of
your growing up, huh?

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.549
-Yeah. I mean,
I grew up in Kentucky, so --

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.551
-Whoo!
-Yeah, thank you.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.285
It's just below
the Mason-Dixon Line.

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:02.621
-That sounded like the wind
going through a holler.

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:05.391
[ Laughter ]
-No, that's Mitch McConnell

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.992
and Joe Biden down there
right now.

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:09.128
Whoo! Whoo!
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.063
-That was like somebody
blowing it over a jug.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:13.165
-Yeah, yeah. Whoo!
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:15.969
But, um, yeah.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.437
So, you know, ostensibly,
I guess,

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:20.473
everybody from the South
is supposed to be

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.009
tremendously familiar
with country music.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:26.779
But, yeah, I -- my only contact
with it, really,

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.747
was seeing it on "Hee Haw."
every now and then.

00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:30.316
-Yeah.
-We'd watch "Hee Haw" on TV.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.085
-So, did that make it easier
to take on playing George Jones,

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:35.221
that you didn't sort of
grow up married to that music?

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:39.392
-Yeah, I suppose so, because --
Yeah, it wasn't like I was

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:42.261
trying to step into
one of my idols' shoes,

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.697
although as I was
researching him

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.132
and getting to know him
better and better,

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:49.802
I became quite fond of him
as you're, I guess, supposed to

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.437
if you're playing somebody.
But, yeah.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.407
[ Light laughter ]
I guess, at the outset,

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:55.808
I didn't really
know enough about it

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:59.378
to be super intimidated.
But the singing

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:01.080
was very intimidating.
-Because you're doing

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.282
your own singing.
-Yes.

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.483
-You're obviously
playing this great vocalist,

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:05.918
not someone
you're familiar with.

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:07.754
And also, I think
people don't understand --

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.922
it's wonderful to watch --
I've loved what I've seen

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.591
so far --
you guys sound great,

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.694
but, of course, to film music,
you've got to sing all day long.

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.897
-Yes, and you have to sing --
ultimately,

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:20.466
to get the good sound
on the track,

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:24.237
you have to sing a cappella,
which means that you have

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:27.873
a little earwig in your ear,
and you can hear the song.

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:29.675
But all the extras
out in the audience

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:31.644
just hear your voice.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.612
So you're singing a cappella

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:35.114
in front of
hundreds of strangers.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:36.315
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:39.552
And there was one particular day
where we had --

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.521
Jessica and I, we had
a daytime outdoor concert,

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.123
and then I had, that same day,

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.692
a nighttime outdoor concert
all by myself.

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:50.596
And it was freezing cold,
and, yeah, we --

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:54.967
I mean, our wonderful
music producer, Rachael Moore,

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:58.070
she said I was out there
singing, you know, 14, 15 hours.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.972
And she kept telling me --

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:02.408
because we did have
prerecorded tracks

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.844
that I could lip-synch to --
and she said, "You know,

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.046
we've already got the track.
Stop. What are you doing?"

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.581
I said, "No, I think
I can do it better."

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.484
So I just kept doing it
over and over and over again.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:14.653
-I really feel like
doing it in front of strangers

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:17.289
is my full nightmare.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:18.925
It would be like
going to a karaoke bar

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.593
where they say,
"The machine's down,

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.728
but we'd still love
to hear you --"

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:24.063
-"But you know the words,
right?"

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:25.565
-"You know the words."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:27.867
That's exactly
basically what it's like.

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.635
-What was the music you
listened to in your house,

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:31.504
growing up?
-Oh, dear.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.606
[ Laughter ]
Well, I mean --

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:37.076
Uh, I-I-I -- We didn't really --

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:40.012
it was more of a TV house
than a stereo house.

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:43.448
I got a boombox around
when I was 12 years old --

00:04:43.448 --> 00:04:46.452
a little tape player --
and the first two tapes I bought

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:49.555
were Phil Collins
"No Jacket Required"...

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:50.756
-Yep, classic.

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.257
-...and Stevie Wonder,
"Part-Time Lover."

00:04:52.257 --> 00:04:54.460
-Really good start, yeah.
-Were my first two cassettes,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.961
Yeah.
And I wore those out.

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:59.632
And then,
when I started high school,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:03.802
I got into the jazz band
and started playing bass --

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:07.906
jazz bass -- and really started
getting into jazz music.

00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:09.308
-Alright.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.678
-I should have asked this
when you first came out,

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:13.179
but what did you do
for New Year's?

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:14.580
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.348
[ Laughter ]
Well, [Chuckles]

00:05:16.348 --> 00:05:20.386
uh, it was -- it was legal.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.989
-Great.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:25.391
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.726
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:27.726 --> 00:05:31.897
-Right now, I'm doing a play
here in New York, actually.

00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:33.566
We got one more week.
It's called "Des Moines,"

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.134
if you find yourself
in Brooklyn.

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:38.671
And anyway, we had two shows
on New Year's Eve

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.073
and two shows on New Year's Day.
-Oh, wow.

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:42.742
So there's not a lot of
partying between there?

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:45.745
-No. After our nighttime show
on New Year's Eve,

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.047
I went to a restaurant
down the street

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.982
and had dinner by myself.
-By yourself?

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.317
-Yeah.
They said, "We're gonna have

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:53.619
a champagne toast at midnight.
Would you like a glass?"

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.455
I said, "No, I'm leaving."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:57.490
And I made sure
that when it was midnight,

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.860
I was walking down the sidewalk.
-Now, are you approached

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.429
when you have dinner alone
on sort of a festive night?

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:05.364
Do you think people say,
"Hey, that's Michael Shannon.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:08.167
I feel awful that he's there"?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.837
-There were people that came up
and said hello, yeah.

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.973
And then I told them to go away.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:17.943
I said, "Mind your own business
and don't feel sorry for me,

00:06:17.943 --> 00:06:19.846
no matter what you do.
I'm fine."

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:24.149
-Before -- Long before you
found your way to George Jones,

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:26.385
obviously, you have
this musical background.

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:28.020
You were in
a high school jazz band.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.289
You also busked
here in New York City.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.091
-[ Laughs ]
Well, it was Chicago.

00:06:32.091 --> 00:06:33.959
-Oh, Chicago, alright.
-This was back when

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:37.196
I was acting for free,
and five people were coming

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:38.631
to see the plays I was in.

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:42.034
And I was trying to figure out
how to get something to eat.

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.203
So I had this little bongo --

00:06:45.203 --> 00:06:47.639
just like two little hooked up
together like that --

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:52.277
and I would go down,
and in the CTA, there's a stop,

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:53.979
the Washington stop,
that has a tunnel

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:55.314
between the Red Line
and the Blue Line.

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:57.283
I would sit in this tunnel
and play the bongos.

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.885
-How did you do?
-Not well.

00:06:59.885 --> 00:07:01.787
-Yeah.
-Not well.

00:07:01.787 --> 00:07:03.355
-Were you good at it?
-It's a sense memory.

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:05.191
It's coming back.
I can't stop myself.

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:08.260
-Oh, wait, wait, don't stop.
-Give me some bongos over here.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:09.962
-A bunch of change is
gonna be chucked up here.

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:11.329
-Yeah, yeah.
-Stick around.

00:07:11.329 --> 00:07:13.533
We'll be right back
with more from Michael Shannon.

